WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.920
Sometimes - like in the case of birds and insects -

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.860
migration is built into the very DNA -

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.520
they migrate because their bodies tell them to.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.440
But for ungulates, such as deer, sheep, and moose,

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.400
it’s long been hypothesized that seasonal migration

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.520
is a learned behavior, passed down

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.100
from one generation to the next.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.920
The question is do they innately know

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.480
to go where the best, greenest, healthiest grazing

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.380
is at different times of the year—

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.500
or do they need to learn from others in the herd?

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.520
Researchers have now put this to the test

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.500
by tracking the movements of bighorn sheep

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.660
and moose in the American West.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.600
They used GPS data of hoofstock

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.060
from both historic populations -- centuries old

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.420
in some cases -- and populations that had

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.540
been moved over the past few decades

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.760
due to overhunting and disease.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.540
The researchers tracked the way these different

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.500
types of herds migrated over 2-4 years

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.600
and compared how their movements matched up

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.360
with the quality of the available food in the area.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.200
Ideally, when ungulates migrate,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.940
they synchronize their movements to forage for

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.220
vegetation when it’s most nutritious

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.140
and easiest to digest -

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.940
a process called “green-wave surfing”.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.180
In the American West,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.900
that means as the weather warms,

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.480
they follow the best fodder from lower elevations

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.500
to higher ones—

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.740
following the best greens up the mountains.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.640
The research team used NASA’s MODIS satellite

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.300
to take snapshots of the greenery over time

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.780
while an onboard spectrometer

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.040
analyzed the forage quality.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.000
Researchers then overlaid the data

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.300
from the animals movements gleaned from GPS collars

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.520
with these maps of forage quality

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.280
They defined an animal’s surfing ability

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.280
as the difference between day forage quality peaked

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.400
and the day the animal foraged at the location.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.060
This map follows three historic herds of bighorn sheep

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.900
in Whiskey Basin, Wyoming

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.280
as the vegetation begins to mature.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.400
Having lived there for centuries, they easily

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.220
follow the vegetation as it blooms up the mountain.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.180
This map, however,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.560
follows three relocated herds of sheep

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.360
85 miles to the north in Devils Canyon, Wyoming.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.300
Since they’ve only lived there for a short time,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.700
they haven’t yet learned to green-wave surf,

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.820
so they stay put.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.000
The results showed that historic populations

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.180
were much better at riding the green wave

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.900
than populations that had recently been relocated—

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.320
suggesting that green-wave surfing migration

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.240
is learned or culturally transmitted.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.000
It’s likely this ability takes decades to develop,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.840
as each generation becomes familiar with the area

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.160
and passes down this knowledge to their offspring.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.480
However, once a species is relocated

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.720
or driven locally extinct due to disease or overhunting,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.180
that knowledge is lost forever

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.740
and could take decades to be restored.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.380
Though this study is only based on a few herds,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.300
the researchers believe that this emphasizes

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.820
the importance of not only establishing

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:50.060
new migration corridors for relocated species,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.100
but also preserving ones that have already existed for,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.120
in some cases, centuries.

